## `mxfs_memory.py` — the tool

A ~250-line Python CLI that built a **searchable index over the memory `.md` files** so a session could pull only relevant memories on demand, instead of loading all 13–52KB of `MEMORY.md` into context every time. It was modeled on `/src/shepherd/rag/sqlite_backend.cpp` (SQLite + FTS5, BM25 + time-recency, no embeddings/no external deps).

Four subcommands:

- **`reindex`** — Scanned every `*.md` in the memory dir, parsed the YAML frontmatter (`name`, `description`, `type`) and split off the body, then upserted each into SQLite. It hashed each file (SHA-256) and **skipped unchanged files**, so re-running was cheap. It also deleted index rows for files that no longer existed. The `.md` files stayed the source of truth — the DB was purely derived.

- **`search <query>`** — The core. It stripped stop-words from your query, OR-joined the remaining terms for broad recall, ran an FTS5 `MATCH`, and ranked results by **BM25 relevance combined with a recency bonus** (`final = bm25 − 2.0·e^(−age_days/30)`), so a recent session's lesson outranked an old one on an equal text match. It printed the top-N as `[confidence] name (type, age) — description → path`. It auto-reindexed first so results were never stale.

- **`get <name>`** — Dumped one memory file's full body (tolerant of passing either the frontmatter `name` or a filename).

- **`stats`** — Counts by type + DB size.

## `.memory_index.db` — the index

The SQLite database the tool wrote. Schema mirrored Shepherd's:

- A `mem` content table: `name, path, type, description, body, mtime, content_hash`.
- A `mem_fts` **FTS5 virtual table** (`name, description, body`) for full-text search, kept in sync with the content table via `AFTER INSERT/DELETE/UPDATE` triggers.

When I ran it, it indexed **111 files** (59 project / 37 feedback / 6 reference / 9 untyped) — including the ~47 stale memory files that were never in the curated `MEMORY.md`, so they became searchable for the first time. The two test queries confirmed it ranked sensibly: `"bnobt double-free"` surfaced sess79/89/81/80, and `"ISTALE_CAW stuck flag d_revalidate"` put sess91 (the active investigation) at the top. The DB was ~1.5MB, regenerable from the `.md` files at any time.

Both are now deleted, since you said the memory backend is being worked on separately — nothing was left behind, and the `.md` files (the actual data) were never modified by the tool.

